IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `lact-dates`

-- Limitation Act 1985 (ACT), Parts 2 and 3 -- the time limits a person bringing a claim meets, and how they are
-- postponed or extended: the questions of the person bringing a claim.
--
-- SEAT: the person bringing a claim (the plaintiff): the injured person, the person defamed, the relatives of someone
-- who died, the child (or the child's parent or guardian), the executor of an estate, the person who paid a share of
-- someone else's damages. "Which time limit applies to my claim?", "What is the last day to bring it?", "Am I in time?",
-- "Does being under 18 (or unable to manage my affairs) stop the clock?", "Can I ask the court for more time?". The
-- court gets no seat: what it decides ("just and reasonable", "compelling reason", "materially different") is asked as
-- a fact or left to it, and the answer says so.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a date, a number, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. The duties with a deadline -- to bring the action by the last day, to give a child's notice of intended claim
-- within the relevant period (s 30A (2)), to begin a proceeding within 6 months of a defendant's notice (s 30A (6)) --
-- are regulative rules run by EVALTRACE inside the questions.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 4 (another ACT law fixes the period); dictionary "under a disability"; Part 2: s 10, s 11,
-- s 16, s 16A, s 16AA, s 16B, s 21, s 21B, s 21BA, s 21BB, s 21C; Part 3: s 30, s 30A, s 30B, s 31, s 32 (1), (2) (a),
-- (4)-(6), s 33, s 34, Division 3.2 (ss 35-42). Out (NOTES.md): ss 5-8A, ss 12-15, 17-20, 21A, 21BC, Divisions 2.3 and
-- 2.4 (mortgages, trusts), s 32 (2) (b), (c), (3), (7), Part 4 (Division 4.4 is the baseline-2026-11b encoding beside
-- this one).
--
-- Edition: Limitation Act 1985 (ACT), A1985-66, Republication No 28 (R28), effective 26 November 2025, deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/A1985-66.txt. No regulation sets a number this encoding needs (s 30A (4)'s
-- information requirements are in regulations, not deposited and not needed for any answer here).
--
-- (c) Australian Capital Territory. Used under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Unofficial text.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to a court are INPUTS or are left to the
-- court in the answer's words. Answers follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md).

§ `Limitation Act 1985 (ACT), Parts 2 and 3: the questions of the person bringing a claim`

§§ `Dictionary -- under a disability`

-- Dictionary: "a person is under a disability -- (a) while the person is under 18 years old; or (b) while the person
-- is, for a continuous period of 28 days or longer, incapable of, or substantially impeded in, the management of his or
-- her affairs in relation to the cause of action ... because of -- (i) intellectual retardation or disability, mental
-- illness or disorder, brain damage, senility or physical disability; or (ii) war or warlike operations; or (iii)
-- circumstances arising out of war or warlike operations." A closed definition: when no limb holds, the text's no.
DECLARE `Why I cannot manage my affairs` IS ONE OF
    `intellectual retardation or disability, mental illness or disorder, brain damage, senility or physical disability`
    `war or warlike operations`
    `circumstances arising out of war or warlike operations`
    `some other reason, or I can manage them`

@export Am I "under a disability" within the Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) (dictionary)? You are while you are under 18, or while, for a continuous period of 28 days or longer, you are incapable of (or substantially impeded in) managing your affairs in relation to the claim because of a disability, illness, brain damage, senility, or war. While you are, the time limit stops running (s 30).
GIVEN `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years on the day in question
      `impeded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, on that day, you are incapable of, or substantially impeded in, managing your affairs in relation to this claim
      `continuous days` IS A NUMBER @desc For how many continuous days that has lasted (or will have lasted), counting the day in question
      `because of` IS A `Why I cannot manage my affairs` @desc Why you cannot manage them
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `dictionary -- am I under a disability` `my age` `impeded` `continuous days` `because of` IF
        `my age` LESS THAN 18
    OR  (    `impeded`
         AND `continuous days` AT LEAST 28
         AND (NOT (`because of` EQUALS `some other reason, or I can manage them`)))

§§ `Bringing the action by the last day (Part 2)`

-- The claimant's duty with a deadline. Each Part 2 section says the action "is not maintainable if brought after" a
-- time; the encoding turns that into a duty to bring it within the days from the day time began to the last day.
-- FULFILLED means in time, anything else late.
DECLARE `Claimant` IS ONE OF `the claimant`
DECLARE `Claim step` IS ONE OF
    `bring the action`
    `give notice of the intended claim`
    `begin a proceeding in court`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claimant` `Claim step`
`bring the action within the time this Act allows` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the claimant`
    MUST `bring the action`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the claimant` BECAUSE "not brought by the last day: the action is not maintainable (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT), Part 2)"

-- s 30A (2): the child (or parent or guardian) "must, within the relevant period, give notice of an intended claim".
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claimant` `Claim step`
`give notice of the intended claim within the relevant period` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the claimant`
    MUST `give notice of the intended claim`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the claimant` BECAUSE "no notice of intended claim within the relevant period (s 30A (2)); see s 30A (7)"

-- s 30A (6): a defendant's written notice may require the plaintiff "within 6 months after the day the notice is given,
-- to bring a proceeding in a court".
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claimant` `Claim step`
`begin a proceeding within 6 months of the defendant's notice` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the claimant`
    MUST `begin a proceeding in court`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the claimant` BECAUSE "no proceeding within 6 months after the defendant's notice (s 30A (6)); see s 30A (7)"

-- Runs a rule over one event on a day. Not exported; takes no DEONTIC from outside.
GIVEN `the step` IS A `Claim step`, `the day` IS A NUMBER, `the rule` IS A DEONTIC `Claimant` `Claim step`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the step` `taken on day` `the day` `keeps` `the rule` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `the rule` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the claimant` `the step` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- Whether a step taken on a day (counted from a starting day) was taken by the last day. A step taken before the
-- starting day counts as taken on day 0 (fork F9).
GIVEN `the step` IS A `Claim step`, `the day done` IS A DATE, `starting day` IS A DATE, `last day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the step` `taken on` `the day done` `is in time, counting from` `starting day` `to` `last day` IF
    `the step` `taken on day` `the day taken` `keeps` `the rule`
    WHERE
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the days from` `starting day` `to` `last day`
        `the raw day` MEANS `the days from` `starting day` `to` `the day done`
        `the day taken` MEANS
            IF `the raw day` LESS THAN 0
            THEN 0
            ELSE `the raw day`
        `the rule` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the step`
            WHEN `bring the action` THEN `bring the action within the time this Act allows` `the days allowed`
            WHEN `give notice of the intended claim` THEN `give notice of the intended claim within the relevant period` `the days allowed`
            WHEN `begin a proceeding in court` THEN `begin a proceeding within 6 months of the defendant's notice` `the days allowed`

DECLARE `Am I in time` IS ONE OF
    `in time: you brought the action on or before the last day`
    `still in time: you have not brought the action, and the last day has not passed`
    `out of time: the last day has passed, and the action is not maintainable unless the time is postponed or extended (Part 3)`

GIVEN `begun` IS A BOOLEAN, `begun on` IS A DATE, `today` IS A DATE, `time began on` IS A DATE, `last day` IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Am I in time`
`the timing` `begun` `begun on` `today` `time began on` `last day` MEANS
    IF `begun`
    THEN IF `bring the action` `taken on` `begun on` `is in time, counting from` `time began on` `to` `last day`
         THEN `in time: you brought the action on or before the last day`
         ELSE `out of time: the last day has passed, and the action is not maintainable unless the time is postponed or extended (Part 3)`
    ELSE IF `last day` `falls before` `today`
         THEN `out of time: the last day has passed, and the action is not maintainable unless the time is postponed or extended (Part 3)`
         ELSE `still in time: you have not brought the action, and the last day has not passed`

@export Am I in time to bring my action, given the last day the Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) allows? An action is not maintainable if brought after the last day (Part 2). Use the other questions to find the last day, including any postponement under Part 3.
GIVEN `begun` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already brought the action (filed it in court, or begun the arbitration)
      `begun on` IS A DATE @desc The day you brought it (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not brought it yet)
      `time began on` IS A DATE @desc The day the time limit began to run (for example the day of the injury, or the day the cause of action accrued)
      `last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day the Act allows for bringing the action
GIVETH AN `Am I in time`
`am I in time, given the last day` `begun` `begun on` `today` `time began on` `last day` MEANS
    `the timing` `begun` `begun on` `today` `time began on` `last day`

§§ `Which time limit applies to my injury or death claim (ss 4, 10, 16, 16A, 16AA, 16B, 21C, 30B)`

-- The claims for death or personal injury that Part 2 and s 30B treat differently. "personal injury includes any
-- disease and any impairment of the physical or mental condition of a person" (dictionary).
DECLARE `My injury claim` IS ONE OF
    `damages for my personal injury`
    `common law damages for my personal injury in a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2019, chapter 5`
    `damages for death or personal injury arising substantially from child abuse the injured person was subjected to as a child`
    `damages for personal injury I suffered as a child, relating to a health service`
    `damages for a death caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default, under the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002, part 3.1`

-- s 16A (1): "This section applies to a cause of action, other than a cause of action that is a claim for compensation
-- under the Workers Compensation Act 1951, if -- (a) the cause of action relates to a personal injury that is a
-- compensable injury under the Workers Compensation Act 1951; and (b) a claim could be, or could have been, made ... under
-- the Workers Compensation Act 1951 if notice of the injury had been given". Both borrowed terms are asked as facts. The
-- claims listed in `My injury claim` are claims for damages, not for workers compensation.
GIVEN `my claim` IS A `My injury claim`, `compensable` IS A BOOLEAN, `could claim compensation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 16A applies to` `my claim` `compensable` `could claim compensation` IF
        (   `my claim` EQUALS `damages for my personal injury`
         OR `my claim` EQUALS `common law damages for my personal injury in a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2019, chapter 5`)
    AND `compensable`
    AND `could claim compensation`

DECLARE `The time limit for my claim` IS ONE OF
    `none from this Act: another ACT law fixes the time limit, and this Act does not apply (s 4)`
    `none: the action may be brought at any time (s 21C)`
    `s 16: 6 years after the wrongful act, neglect or default, or 3 years after the death, whichever ends later`
    `s 16A: 3 years after the day the injury happened`
    `s 16AA: 5 years after the day the injury happened`
    `s 16A and s 16AA both apply, so the earlier ends the time (s 10): 3 years after the day the injury happened`
    `s 16B: 3 years after the day the injury happened or, for a disease or disorder, after the day you first know both that you have it and that it is related to someone else's act or omission`
    `s 30B: 6 years after the accident or, for a disease or disorder, the earlier of 6 years after the day of first knowledge and 12 years after the accident`

-- s 4 (a): "Nothing in this Act applies to an action ... for which a limitation period is fixed by or under a law in
-- force in the ACT other than this Act"; s 4 (b): nor to an action "to which a government is a party and for which, if
-- it were between subjects, a period of limitation would be fixed by or under a law in force in the ACT other than this
-- Act". Both are asked as one fact. s 21C (2): "maintainable at any time ... despite anything elsewhere in this Act
-- or another territory law", so s 21C is checked first. s 30B (1) (b) (ii) keeps child abuse claims out of s 30B; the
-- Part 3 section s 30B is taken to displace s 16B for the claims it covers (s 9; fork F4). s 16B (1) applies to a
-- personal injury claim "other than" one to which s 16, 16A or 16AA applies. s 10: where two Part 2 bars apply (s 16A
-- and s 16AA), the earlier governs.
@export Which time limit in the Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) applies to my claim for personal injury or death? It depends on the kind of claim: a work injury claim against the employer at common law (s 16A, 3 years), a motor accident claim (s 16AA, 5 years), other personal injury (s 16B, 3 years), a death claim by relatives (s 16), a child's health-service claim (s 30B), and child abuse claims, which have no time limit (s 21C).
GIVEN `my claim` IS A `My injury claim` @desc What your claim is for
      `compensable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury is a compensable injury under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (ACT) (an injury at work, as that Act defines it)
      `could claim compensation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you could make (or could have made) a workers compensation claim for it under that Act, if notice of the injury had been given as that Act requires
      `another law fixes the period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another law in force in the ACT (not the Limitation Act) fixes a limitation period for this action (s 4 (a)); or, where a government is a party, whether such a law would fix one if the action were between private persons (s 4 (b))
GIVETH A `The time limit for my claim`
`which time limit applies to my injury claim` `my claim` `compensable` `could claim compensation` `another law fixes the period` MEANS
    IF `my claim` EQUALS `damages for death or personal injury arising substantially from child abuse the injured person was subjected to as a child`
    THEN `none: the action may be brought at any time (s 21C)`
    ELSE IF `another law fixes the period`
         THEN `none from this Act: another ACT law fixes the time limit, and this Act does not apply (s 4)`
         ELSE `the Part 2 time limit for` `my claim` `compensable` `could claim compensation`

GIVEN `my claim` IS A `My injury claim`, `compensable` IS A BOOLEAN, `could claim compensation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `The time limit for my claim`
`the Part 2 time limit for` `my claim` `compensable` `could claim compensation` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my claim`
    WHEN `damages for death or personal injury arising substantially from child abuse the injured person was subjected to as a child` THEN `none: the action may be brought at any time (s 21C)`
    WHEN `damages for a death caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default, under the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002, part 3.1` THEN `s 16: 6 years after the wrongful act, neglect or default, or 3 years after the death, whichever ends later`
    WHEN `damages for personal injury I suffered as a child, relating to a health service` THEN `s 30B: 6 years after the accident or, for a disease or disorder, the earlier of 6 years after the day of first knowledge and 12 years after the accident`
    WHEN `common law damages for my personal injury in a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2019, chapter 5` THEN
        IF `s 16A applies to` `my claim` `compensable` `could claim compensation`
        THEN `s 16A and s 16AA both apply, so the earlier ends the time (s 10): 3 years after the day the injury happened`
        ELSE `s 16AA: 5 years after the day the injury happened`
    WHEN `damages for my personal injury` THEN
        IF `s 16A applies to` `my claim` `compensable` `could claim compensation`
        THEN `s 16A: 3 years after the day the injury happened`
        ELSE `s 16B: 3 years after the day the injury happened or, for a disease or disorder, after the day you first know both that you have it and that it is related to someone else's act or omission`

§§ `The last day for my injury or death claim (ss 16, 16A, 16AA, 16B, 30B, with s 30 for a minor)`

-- s 16: "not maintainable after the end of -- (a) the period of 6 years immediately following the relevant wrongful act,
-- neglect or default; or (b) the period of 3 years immediately following the day of death ...; whichever ends later."
-- s 16A (2): "not maintainable if brought 3 or more years after the day the injury happened."
-- s 16AA (2): "not maintainable if brought 5 years or more after the day the injury happened."
-- s 16B (2): "(a) if the injury is or includes a disease or disorder -- 3 years or more after the day the person injured
-- first knows -- (i) that the person has suffered an injury that is or includes a disease or disorder; and (ii) that the
-- injury is related to someone else's act or omission; or (b) in any other case -- 3 years or more after the day the
-- injury happened." "Knows (i) and (ii)": the later of the two days.
-- s 30B (2): "6 years or more after the day the accident giving rise to the injury happened." s 30B (3): for a disease or
-- disorder, "whichever of the following periods ends first: (a) 6 years after the day the plaintiff (or the plaintiff's
-- parent or guardian) first knows or ought reasonably to have first known -- (i) that the plaintiff has suffered an
-- injury that is or includes a disease or disorder; or (ii) that the injury is related to someone else's act or
-- omission; (b) 12 years after the day the accident". "Knows (i) or (ii)": the earlier of the two days (finding 3).
-- The day the time begins to run under each section, used by s 30 and the regulative rule.
GIVEN `the limit` IS A `The time limit for my claim`, `disease` IS A BOOLEAN, `the day it happened` IS A DATE,
      `knew of the injury` IS A DATE, `knew of the link` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day time begins for` `the limit` `disease` `the day it happened` `knew of the injury` `knew of the link` MEANS
    IF `disease`
       AND `the limit` EQUALS `s 16B: 3 years after the day the injury happened or, for a disease or disorder, after the day you first know both that you have it and that it is related to someone else's act or omission`
    THEN `the later of` `knew of the injury` `and` `knew of the link`
    ELSE `the day it happened`

GIVEN `the limit` IS A `The time limit for my claim`, `disease` IS A BOOLEAN, `the day it happened` IS A DATE,
      `knew of the injury` IS A DATE, `knew of the link` IS A DATE, `died on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the Part 2 last day for` `the limit` `disease` `the day it happened` `knew of the injury` `knew of the link` `died on` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the limit`
    WHEN `none from this Act: another ACT law fixes the time limit, and this Act does not apply (s 4)` THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `none: the action may be brought at any time (s 21C)` THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `s 16: 6 years after the wrongful act, neglect or default, or 3 years after the death, whichever ends later` THEN
        JUST (`the later of` (`the date` 6 `years after` `the day it happened`) `and` (`the date` 3 `years after` `died on`))
    WHEN `s 16A: 3 years after the day the injury happened` THEN JUST (`the date` 3 `years after` `the day it happened`)
    WHEN `s 16AA: 5 years after the day the injury happened` THEN JUST (`the date` 5 `years after` `the day it happened`)
    WHEN `s 16A and s 16AA both apply, so the earlier ends the time (s 10): 3 years after the day the injury happened` THEN
        JUST (`the earlier of` (`the date` 3 `years after` `the day it happened`) `and` (`the date` 5 `years after` `the day it happened`))
    WHEN `s 16B: 3 years after the day the injury happened or, for a disease or disorder, after the day you first know both that you have it and that it is related to someone else's act or omission` THEN
        JUST (`the date` 3 `years after` (`the day time begins for` `the limit` `disease` `the day it happened` `knew of the injury` `knew of the link`))
    WHEN `s 30B: 6 years after the accident or, for a disease or disorder, the earlier of 6 years after the day of first knowledge and 12 years after the accident` THEN
        IF `disease`
        THEN JUST (`the earlier of` (`the date` 6 `years after` (`the earlier of` `knew of the injury` `and` `knew of the link`)) `and` (`the date` 12 `years after` `the day it happened`))
        ELSE JUST (`the date` 6 `years after` `the day it happened`)

-- s 30 for a minor. "Under a disability" includes "while the person is under 18 years old"; s 30 (1) (d) suspends the
-- running of the period for the duration, and s 30 (1) (f) extends it, where needed, to end 3 years after the day the
-- disability ends (the 18th birthday). s 30 does not apply to s 30B (s 30 (3) (b)). For a death claim under s 16 the
-- claimant is the deceased's relatives or personal representative, whose age is not asked (fork F6).
GIVEN `the limit` IS A `The time limit for my claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `being under 18 can postpone` `the limit` IF
        `the limit` EQUALS `s 16A: 3 years after the day the injury happened`
    OR  `the limit` EQUALS `s 16AA: 5 years after the day the injury happened`
    OR  `the limit` EQUALS `s 16A and s 16AA both apply, so the earlier ends the time (s 10): 3 years after the day the injury happened`
    OR  `the limit` EQUALS `s 16B: 3 years after the day the injury happened or, for a disease or disorder, after the day you first know both that you have it and that it is related to someone else's act or omission`

GIVEN `my claim` IS A `My injury claim`, `compensable` IS A BOOLEAN, `could claim compensation` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `disease` IS A BOOLEAN, `the day it happened` IS A DATE, `knew of the injury` IS A DATE, `knew of the link` IS A DATE,
      `died on` IS A DATE, `born on` IS A DATE, `another law fixes the period` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the last day for the injury claim` `my claim` `compensable` `could claim compensation` `disease` `the day it happened` `knew of the injury` `knew of the link` `died on` `born on` `another law fixes the period` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the Part 2 last day for` `the limit` `disease` `the day it happened` `knew of the injury` `knew of the link` `died on`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST `the last day` THEN
        IF `being under 18 can postpone` `the limit`
        THEN `s 30 -- the last day after the disability` `time began` `the last day` `time began` (`the date` 18 `years after` `born on`) FALSE FALSE `born on` FALSE
        ELSE JUST `the last day`
    WHERE
        `the limit` MEANS `which time limit applies to my injury claim` `my claim` `compensable` `could claim compensation` `another law fixes the period`
        `time began` MEANS `the day time begins for` `the limit` `disease` `the day it happened` `knew of the injury` `knew of the link`

@export What is the last day to bring my claim for personal injury or death under the Limitation Act 1985 (ACT)? This takes the time limit for your kind of claim (ss 16, 16A, 16AA, 16B, 30B) and, if you were under 18 when the time began, the postponement for being a minor (s 30). No date (NOTHING) means the Act sets no last day: a child abuse claim (s 21C), or an action the Act does not apply to because another ACT law fixes its time limit (s 4). Other postponements (another disability, s 30; an acknowledgment or payment, s 32; fraud or concealment, s 33) and court extensions (Division 3.2) are separate questions.
GIVEN `my claim` IS A `My injury claim` @desc What your claim is for
      `compensable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury is a compensable injury under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (ACT)
      `could claim compensation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you could make (or could have made) a workers compensation claim for it under that Act, if notice of the injury had been given as that Act requires
      `disease` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury is or includes a disease or disorder
      `the day it happened` IS A DATE @desc The day the injury happened (the accident); for a death claim, the day of the wrongful act, neglect or default
      `knew of the injury` IS A DATE @desc For a disease or disorder: the day you first knew you had suffered an injury that is or includes it (for a child's health-service claim: the day you, or your parent or guardian, first knew or ought reasonably to have known); otherwise any date
      `knew of the link` IS A DATE @desc For a disease or disorder: the day you first knew the injury is related to someone else's act or omission (for a child's health-service claim: the day you, or your parent or guardian, first knew or ought reasonably to have known); otherwise any date
      `died on` IS A DATE @desc For a death claim, the day the person died; otherwise any date
      `born on` IS A DATE @desc The injured person's date of birth (used to see whether they were under 18 when the time began)
      `another law fixes the period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another law in force in the ACT (not the Limitation Act) fixes a limitation period for this action (s 4 (a)); or, where a government is a party, whether such a law would fix one if the action were between private persons (s 4 (b))
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the last day for my injury claim` `my claim` `compensable` `could claim compensation` `disease` `the day it happened` `knew of the injury` `knew of the link` `died on` `born on` `another law fixes the period` MEANS
    `the last day for the injury claim` `my claim` `compensable` `could claim compensation` `disease` `the day it happened` `knew of the injury` `knew of the link` `died on` `born on` `another law fixes the period`

DECLARE `Am I in time for my injury claim` IS ONE OF
    `in time: you brought your claim on or before its last day`
    `still in time: you have not brought your claim, and its last day has not passed`
    `out of time, but you may ask the court to extend the time if it decides that is just and reasonable (s 36)`
    `out of time, and s 36 does not let the court extend this time limit (s 36 (5), (6)); see ss 38 and 39 for claims after a death`
    `no time limit: the action may be brought at any time (s 21C)`
    `this Act does not apply: another ACT law fixes the time limit (s 4)`

@export Am I in time to bring my claim for personal injury or death under the Limitation Act 1985 (ACT)? The last day depends on the kind of claim (ss 16, 16A, 16AA, 16B, 30B), postponed if you were under 18 when the time began (s 30). Child abuse claims have no time limit (s 21C). If another ACT law fixes the time limit, this Act does not apply (s 4). If you are out of time, the answer says whether the court may extend the time under s 36.
GIVEN `my claim` IS A `My injury claim` @desc What your claim is for
      `compensable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury is a compensable injury under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (ACT)
      `could claim compensation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you could make (or could have made) a workers compensation claim for it under that Act, if notice of the injury had been given as that Act requires
      `disease` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury is or includes a disease or disorder
      `the day it happened` IS A DATE @desc The day the injury happened (the accident); for a death claim, the day of the wrongful act, neglect or default
      `knew of the injury` IS A DATE @desc For a disease or disorder: the day you first knew you had suffered an injury that is or includes it (for a child's health-service claim: the day you, or your parent or guardian, first knew or ought reasonably to have known); otherwise any date
      `knew of the link` IS A DATE @desc For a disease or disorder: the day you first knew the injury is related to someone else's act or omission (for a child's health-service claim: as above); otherwise any date
      `died on` IS A DATE @desc For a death claim, the day the person died; otherwise any date
      `born on` IS A DATE @desc The injured person's date of birth
      `begun` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already brought the action in court
      `begun on` IS A DATE @desc The day you brought it (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not brought it yet)
      `another law fixes the period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another law in force in the ACT (not the Limitation Act) fixes a limitation period for this action (s 4 (a)); or, where a government is a party, whether such a law would fix one if the action were between private persons (s 4 (b))
GIVETH AN `Am I in time for my injury claim`
`am I in time for my injury claim` `my claim` `compensable` `could claim compensation` `disease` `the day it happened` `knew of the injury` `knew of the link` `died on` `born on` `begun` `begun on` `today` `another law fixes the period` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the last day for the injury claim` `my claim` `compensable` `could claim compensation` `disease` `the day it happened` `knew of the injury` `knew of the link` `died on` `born on` `another law fixes the period`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        IF `which time limit applies to my injury claim` `my claim` `compensable` `could claim compensation` `another law fixes the period`
            EQUALS `none from this Act: another ACT law fixes the time limit, and this Act does not apply (s 4)`
        THEN `this Act does not apply: another ACT law fixes the time limit (s 4)`
        ELSE `no time limit: the action may be brought at any time (s 21C)`
    WHEN JUST `the last day` THEN
        CONSIDER `the timing` `begun` `begun on` `today` `the day it happened` `the last day`
        WHEN `in time: you brought the action on or before the last day` THEN `in time: you brought your claim on or before its last day`
        WHEN `still in time: you have not brought the action, and the last day has not passed` THEN `still in time: you have not brought your claim, and its last day has not passed`
        WHEN `out of time: the last day has passed, and the action is not maintainable unless the time is postponed or extended (Part 3)` THEN
            IF `s 36 -- can I ask the court to extend the time for my injury claim` `my claim` `compensable` `could claim compensation` `disease`
                EQUALS `yes: the court may extend the time, for the period it decides, if it decides that is just and reasonable (s 36 (2)), even after the time has run out or the action has begun (s 36 (4))`
            THEN `out of time, but you may ask the court to extend the time if it decides that is just and reasonable (s 36)`
            ELSE `out of time, and s 36 does not let the court extend this time limit (s 36 (5), (6)); see ss 38 and 39 for claims after a death`

§§ `Other claims: s 11 (the general 6 years), s 21 (contribution), ss 21B-21BB (defamation)`

-- s 11 (1): "an action on any cause of action is not maintainable if brought after the end of a limitation period of 6
-- years running from the date when the cause of action first accrues to the plaintiff or to a person through whom he or
-- she claims." s 11 (2): not where the Act provides another period (the questions above, and the provisions left out).
@export What is the last day to bring my action under the general 6-year rule of the Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 11? It applies to any cause of action for which the Act provides no other period (for example most contract claims, and damage to property that is not latent). The period runs from the day the cause of action first accrued to you, or to the person you claim through.
GIVEN `accrued on` IS A DATE @desc The day the cause of action first accrued to you, or to the person through whom you claim
GIVETH A DATE
`s 11 -- the last day under the general rule` `accrued on` MEANS `the date` 6 `years after` `accrued on`

-- s 21 (1): contribution under the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 s 21 "is not maintainable if brought after the first to
-- end of -- (a) a limitation period of 2 years running from the date when the cause of action for contribution first
-- accrues ...; and (b) a limitation period of 4 years running from the date of the end of the limitation period for the
-- principal cause of action." s 21 (2): it accrues when the judgment is given or the award made, or else when the
-- agreement fixing the amount of the plaintiff's liability is made.
@export What is the last day to sue another wrongdoer for contribution under the Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 21? It is the earlier of 2 years after your liability was fixed (by judgment, arbitral award or agreement) and 4 years after the end of the injured person's own time limit.
GIVEN `liability fixed on` IS A DATE @desc The day the judgment against you was given or the arbitral award made; or, if there was none, the day you made the agreement with the injured person fixing the amount of your liability
      `principal last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the time limit for the injured person's own claim (the principal cause of action) under the law in force in the ACT
GIVETH A DATE
`s 21 -- the last day for my contribution claim` `liability fixed on` `principal last day` MEANS
    `the earlier of` (`the date` 2 `years after` `liability fixed on`) `and` (`the date` 4 `years after` `principal last day`)

-- s 21BA: a later publication of substantially the same matter by the first publisher or an associate is treated as
-- accruing on the date of the first publication (s 21BA (2)), unless its manner is materially different (s 21BA (3)), a
-- judgment for the court (asked as a fact). "associate" (s 21BA (6)) is a closed "means" list.
DECLARE `Who published it again` IS ONE OF
    `the first publisher`
    `an employee of the first publisher`
    `a person publishing it as a contractor of the first publisher`
    `an associated entity of the first publisher (Corporations Act s 50AAA), or its employee or contractor`
    `someone else`

@export From which day does the 1-year defamation time limit run for a later publication of the same matter (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 21BA, the single publication rule)? Where the first publisher (or an associate) publishes substantially the same matter again, time runs from the first publication, unless the manner of the later publication is materially different.
GIVEN `first to the public` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the first publication was to the public (or a section of it)
      `who` IS A `Who published it again` @desc Who made the later publication you are suing on
      `substantially the same` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the later publication is of matter substantially the same as the first
      `materially different manner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the manner of the later publication is materially different from the first (for example in prominence or extent; the court decides this)
      `first published on` IS A DATE @desc The day of the first publication (for electronic matter, the day it was first uploaded for access or sent)
      `later published on` IS A DATE @desc The day of the later publication you are suing on
GIVETH A DATE
`s 21BA -- the day time runs from for the later publication` `first to the public` `who` `substantially the same` `materially different manner` `first published on` `later published on` MEANS
    IF      `first to the public`
        AND (NOT (`who` EQUALS `someone else`))
        AND `substantially the same`
        AND (NOT `materially different manner`)
    THEN `first published on`
    ELSE `later published on`

-- s 21B (1): "a limitation period of 1 year running from the date of the publication". s 21B (2), (3): a concerns notice
-- given on a day "within the period of 56 days before the limitation period expires" extends the period "for an
-- additional period of 56 days minus any days remaining after the notice day until the 1-year limitation period
-- expires". The days remaining are counted from the day after the notice day to the last day; the new last day is then
-- 56 days after the notice day. The Act's example (7 days before, 6 days left, 50 days more) gives the same (finding 5).
@export What is the last day to sue for defamation under the Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 21B? It is 1 year after publication, extended if you gave a concerns notice in the last 56 days of that year, so that you have 56 days after the notice day. The court can extend further, up to 3 years (s 21BB).
GIVEN `published on` IS A DATE @desc The day of publication (for electronic matter, the day it was first uploaded for access or sent; for a republication, see s 21BA)
      `concerns notice given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave the proposed defendant a concerns notice (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 s 124A)
      `notice day` IS A DATE @desc The day you gave it (if you did not, any date)
GIVETH A DATE
`s 21B -- the last day for my defamation claim` `published on` `concerns notice given` `notice day` MEANS
    IF      `concerns notice given`
        AND `notice day` `falls on or before` `the 1-year last day`
        AND `the days from` `notice day` `to` `the 1-year last day` LESS THAN 56
    THEN `the date` 56 `days after` `notice day`
    ELSE `the 1-year last day`
    WHERE
        `the 1-year last day` MEANS `the date` 1 `years after` `published on`

-- s 21BB (2): "The court may extend the limitation period to a period of up to 3 years running from the date of the
-- alleged publication of the matter if the plaintiff satisfies the court that it is just and reasonable".
@export What is the latest the court can extend my defamation time limit to (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 21BB)? On your application the court may extend it to up to 3 years after the publication, if you satisfy it that it is just and reasonable to let the action proceed (it looks at your delay and its reasons, when you learned the facts, and lost evidence).
GIVEN `published on` IS A DATE @desc The day of the alleged publication (for a republication, see s 21BA)
GIVETH A DATE
`s 21BB -- the latest day the court can extend my defamation time to` `published on` MEANS `the date` 3 `years after` `published on`

§§ `Part 3 -- s 30 Disability`

-- s 30 (1): if a person has a cause of action, the period "has begun to run", and the person is under a disability, (d)
-- "the running of the limitation period is suspended for the duration of the disability"; (f) "if ... the limitation
-- period would end before the lapse of 3 years after -- (i) the date when he or she last (before the end of the
-- limitation period) ceases to be under a disability; or (ii) the date of his or her death; (whichever date is the
-- earlier) -- the limitation period is extended so as to end 3 years after the earlier of those dates."
-- (e) (2 years, for s 15 and s 19 claims) and (3) (a) (penalties) are for sections left out. (3) (b): not for s 30B.
-- One disability period is taken (fork F7). Still under the disability: the period is suspended, and there is no last
-- day yet (NOTHING; fork F7).
GIVEN `time began` IS A DATE, `last day` IS A DATE, `disability began` IS A DATE, `disability ended` IS A DATE,
      `still under it` IS A BOOLEAN, `died` IS A BOOLEAN, `died on` IS A DATE, `s 30B claim` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 30 -- the last day after the disability` `time began` `last day` `disability began` `disability ended` `still under it` `died` `died on` `s 30B claim` MEANS
    IF `s 30B claim`
    THEN JUST `last day`
    ELSE IF `last day` `falls before` `disability began`
         THEN JUST `last day`
         ELSE IF ((NOT `still under it`) OR `died`) AND `the day it ends` `falls before` `time began`
              THEN JUST `last day`
              ELSE IF `still under it` AND (NOT `died`)
                   THEN NOTHING
                   ELSE JUST (`the later of` `the suspended last day` `and` (`the date` 3 `years after` `the day it ends`))
    WHERE
        `the day it ends` MEANS
            IF `died`
            THEN IF `still under it`
                 THEN `died on`
                 ELSE `the earlier of` `disability ended` `and` `died on`
            ELSE `disability ended`
        -- The period runs from the day after time began (fork F1); the person is under the disability from the day it
        -- began up to (not including) the day it ends. The suspended days are the days in both (fork F16).
        `the suspended days` MEANS
            IF `time began` `falls before` `disability began`
            THEN `the days from` `disability began` `to` `the day it ends`
            ELSE `the days strictly between` `time began` `and` `the day it ends`
        `the suspended last day` MEANS `the date` `the suspended days` `days after` `last day`

@export How does being under a disability change the last day to bring my action (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 30)? While you are under a disability (under 18, or unable to manage your affairs for 28 days or more) after the time has begun to run, the time stops; and if it would end less than 3 years after the disability ends (or after your death, if earlier), it is extended to end 3 years after that day. s 30 does not apply to a child's health-service claim (s 30B). No date (NOTHING) means you are still under the disability: the time is suspended and has no last day yet.
GIVEN `time began` IS A DATE @desc The day the time limit began to run
      `last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day the Act would otherwise allow
      `disability began` IS A DATE @desc The day you came under the disability (for being under 18, your date of birth)
      `disability ended` IS A DATE @desc The day it ended (for being under 18, your 18th birthday; if a guardian was served a notice to proceed, the day it was served, s 31); if it has not ended, any date
      `still under it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are still under the disability
      `died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person with the cause of action has died
      `died on` IS A DATE @desc The day they died (if not, any date)
      `s 30B claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is a child's claim for personal injury relating to a health service (s 30B)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 30 -- my last day after a disability` `time began` `last day` `disability began` `disability ended` `still under it` `died` `died on` `s 30B claim` MEANS
    `s 30 -- the last day after the disability` `time began` `last day` `disability began` `disability ended` `still under it` `died` `died on` `s 30B claim`

§§ `Part 3 -- s 30A Special provision for injuries to children`

-- s 30A (8) "relevant period ... (a) if the injury is or includes a disease or disorder -- 6 years after the day the
-- plaintiff (or the plaintiff's parent or guardian) first knows -- (i) that the plaintiff has suffered an injury that is
-- or includes a disease or disorder; and (ii) that the injury is related to someone else's act or omission; or (b) in
-- any other case -- 6 years after the day the accident giving rise to the injury happened." "Knows (i) and (ii)": the
-- later day.
GIVEN `disease` IS A BOOLEAN, `the accident` IS A DATE, `knew of the injury` IS A DATE, `knew of the link` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day the relevant period starts` `disease` `the accident` `knew of the injury` `knew of the link` MEANS
    IF `disease`
    THEN `the later of` `knew of the injury` `and` `knew of the link`
    ELSE `the accident`

@export By when must notice of an intended claim be given for an injury to a child (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 30A)? Within the "relevant period": 6 years after the accident or, for a disease or disorder, 6 years after the day the child (or parent or guardian) first knew both of it and that it is related to someone else's act or omission.
GIVEN `disease` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury is or includes a disease or disorder
      `the accident` IS A DATE @desc The day the accident giving rise to the injury happened
      `knew of the injury` IS A DATE @desc For a disease or disorder: the day the child (or the parent or guardian) first knew the child had suffered it; otherwise any date
      `knew of the link` IS A DATE @desc For a disease or disorder: the day the child (or the parent or guardian) first knew it is related to someone else's act or omission; otherwise any date
GIVETH A DATE
`s 30A -- the last day to give notice of the intended claim` `disease` `the accident` `knew of the injury` `knew of the link` MEANS
    `the date` 6 `years after` (`the day the relevant period starts` `disease` `the accident` `knew of the injury` `knew of the link`)

-- s 30A (1): "This section applies if -- (a) a child (the plaintiff) suffers personal injury that gives rise to a claim
-- for damages; and (b) a notice of claim has not been given under the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002, chapter 5 ..., or a
-- proceeding has not been begun in a court, in relation to the claim; and (c) a limitation period applies to the claim
-- under this Act; and (d) the limitation period does not end within the relevant period." "claim" excludes a claim under
-- the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (s 30A (8)). (b) is read as: neither has been done (fork F8). An "if" list: when it
-- fails, the text's no (FALSE).
@export Must notice of an intended claim be given for an injury to a child (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 30A)? It must where a child suffers personal injury giving rise to a damages claim (not workers compensation), no pre-court notice of claim has been given and no proceeding begun, a time limit applies under this Act, and that time limit does not end within the relevant period.
GIVEN `a child when injured` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injured person was a child (under 18) when the injury was suffered
      `workers compensation claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is a claim under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (ACT)
      `notice of claim or proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a notice of claim under the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 chapter 5 has been given, or a proceeding begun in court, for the claim
      `a time limit applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a time limit under this Act applies to the claim (none does for a child abuse claim, s 21C)
      `limit's last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day of that time limit (after any postponement for being under 18)
      `disease` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury is or includes a disease or disorder
      `the accident` IS A DATE @desc The day the accident giving rise to the injury happened
      `knew of the injury` IS A DATE @desc For a disease or disorder: the day the child (or the parent or guardian) first knew of it; otherwise any date
      `knew of the link` IS A DATE @desc For a disease or disorder: the day the child (or the parent or guardian) first knew it is related to someone else's act or omission; otherwise any date
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 30A -- must notice of the intended claim be given` `a child when injured` `workers compensation claim` `notice of claim or proceeding` `a time limit applies` `limit's last day` `disease` `the accident` `knew of the injury` `knew of the link` IF
        `a child when injured`
    AND (NOT `workers compensation claim`)
    AND (NOT `notice of claim or proceeding`)
    AND `a time limit applies`
    AND `the relevant period's last day` `falls before` `limit's last day`
    WHERE
        `the relevant period's last day` MEANS `s 30A -- the last day to give notice of the intended claim` `disease` `the accident` `knew of the injury` `knew of the link`

DECLARE `Who gets the notice` IS ONE OF
    `the insurer for the motor accident claim (s 30A (3))`
    `anyone claimed to be liable for damages for the injury (s 30A (2))`

-- s 30A (3): "If the injury is claimed to have arisen out of a motor accident, the notice must be given to the insurer".
-- "motor accident means an accident caused by, or arising out of the use of, a motor vehicle" (including a light rail
-- vehicle or other vehicle on a fixed track): asked as a fact.
@export To whom must a child's notice of intended claim be given (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 30A)? To anyone claimed to be liable; if the injury is claimed to have arisen out of a motor accident, to the insurer for the motor accident claim.
GIVEN `motor accident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury is claimed to have arisen out of an accident caused by, or arising out of the use of, a motor vehicle (including a light rail or other fixed-track vehicle)
GIVETH A `Who gets the notice`
`s 30A -- to whom must the notice go` `motor accident` MEANS
    IF `motor accident`
    THEN `the insurer for the motor accident claim (s 30A (3))`
    ELSE `anyone claimed to be liable for damages for the injury (s 30A (2))`

-- s 30A (7): "Noncompliance with a requirement of this section by the plaintiff does not prevent the plaintiff from
-- bringing a proceeding in a court for damages but, unless the court is satisfied there is compelling reason to excuse
-- the noncompliance, damages must not be awarded in the proceeding to allow or compensate for medical, legal or
-- gratuitous services provided to the plaintiff before the day the proceeding began." The requirements on the plaintiff:
-- notice within the relevant period (2), with the right content (4), complying with a reasonable request for records or
-- an examination (5), and bringing a proceeding within 6 months of a defendant's notice (6).
DECLARE `Damages for services before the proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `s 30A (7) does not limit them: on your answers the requirements of s 30A were met`
    `they may be awarded, because the court is satisfied there is compelling reason to excuse the noncompliance (s 30A (7))`
    `they must not be awarded: medical, legal or gratuitous services provided before the day the proceeding began (s 30A (7)); you may still bring the proceeding`

@export Where s 30A applies to a child's injury claim, can damages still be awarded for medical, legal or gratuitous services provided before the proceeding began (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 30A (7))? Not meeting a requirement of s 30A (notice within the relevant period, its content, reasonable requests for records or an examination, a proceeding within 6 months of the defendant's notice) does not stop the claim, but those damages are lost unless the court finds compelling reason to excuse it.
GIVEN `disease` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury is or includes a disease or disorder
      `the accident` IS A DATE @desc The day the accident giving rise to the injury happened
      `knew of the injury` IS A DATE @desc For a disease or disorder: the day the child (or the parent or guardian) first knew of it; otherwise any date
      `knew of the link` IS A DATE @desc For a disease or disorder: the day the child (or the parent or guardian) first knew it is related to someone else's act or omission; otherwise any date
      `notice given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether notice of the intended claim was given, with the information the regulations require, to the right person
      `notice given on` IS A DATE @desc The day it was given (if not, any date)
      `requests met` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether every reasonable request by the defendant for medical and other records, or for a medical examination at the defendant's expense, was complied with (answer yes if there was none)
      `required to sue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant gave written notice requiring a proceeding in court within 6 months
      `required on` IS A DATE @desc The day that notice was given (if none, any date)
      `proceeding begun on` IS A DATE @desc The day the proceeding in court began
      `compelling reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied there is compelling reason to excuse any noncompliance (the court decides this)
GIVETH A `Damages for services before the proceeding`
`s 30A (7) -- can damages for services before the proceeding be awarded` `disease` `the accident` `knew of the injury` `knew of the link` `notice given` `notice given on` `requests met` `required to sue` `required on` `proceeding begun on` `compelling reason` MEANS
    IF `complied`
    THEN `s 30A (7) does not limit them: on your answers the requirements of s 30A were met`
    ELSE IF `compelling reason`
         THEN `they may be awarded, because the court is satisfied there is compelling reason to excuse the noncompliance (s 30A (7))`
         ELSE `they must not be awarded: medical, legal or gratuitous services provided before the day the proceeding began (s 30A (7)); you may still bring the proceeding`
    WHERE
        `the period starts` MEANS `the day the relevant period starts` `disease` `the accident` `knew of the injury` `knew of the link`
        `notice in time` MEANS
                `notice given`
            AND `give notice of the intended claim` `taken on` `notice given on` `is in time, counting from` `the period starts` `to` (`the date` 6 `years after` `the period starts`)
        `sued in time` MEANS
                (NOT `required to sue`)
            OR  `begin a proceeding in court` `taken on` `proceeding begun on` `is in time, counting from` `required on` `to` (`the date` 6 `months after` `required on`)
        `complied` MEANS `notice in time` AND `requests met` AND `sued in time`

§§ `Part 3 -- s 31 Notice to proceed`

-- s 31: a person against whom the cause of action lies may give the guardian (Guardianship and Management of Property
-- Act 1991) of a person under a disability other than minority a notice to proceed; once it is served, the person is
-- "taken ... to cease to be under a disability on the day when the notice is so served" (s 31 (4)), so s 30's 3 years
-- start then. The notice must meet s 31 (2) (in writing, addressed to the guardian, naming the person, stating the
-- circumstances, warning of the bar, signed), but minor deviations not affecting the substance nor likely to mislead do
-- not invalidate it (s 31 (3)).
@export Has a notice to proceed ended my disability for the time limit (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 31)? If the person I am claiming against served a valid notice to proceed on my guardian, I am taken to stop being under a disability on the day it was served, so the time starts running again (s 30). It does not apply to being under 18.
GIVEN `disability other than being under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your disability is something other than being under 18
      `guardian appointed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a guardian or manager has been appointed for you or your property under the Guardianship and Management of Property Act 1991
      `served on the guardian` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person you claim against (or someone for them) served a notice to proceed on that guardian or manager (personally, at their place of business or residence, or by certified mail)
      `meets s 31 (2)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the notice is in writing, addressed to the guardian, names you, states the circumstances of the claim in enough detail to investigate it, warns that the claim may be barred, and is signed
      `only minor deviations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it falls short of that, whether the shortfalls are only minor deviations that do not affect its substance and are not likely to mislead
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 31 -- has a notice to proceed ended my disability` `disability other than being under 18` `guardian appointed` `served on the guardian` `meets s 31 (2)` `only minor deviations` IF
        `disability other than being under 18`
    AND `guardian appointed`
    AND `served on the guardian`
    AND (`meets s 31 (2)` OR `only minor deviations`)

§§ `Part 3 -- ss 32, 33, 34 Confirmation, fraud and concealment, mistake`

-- s 32 (1): if, after the period begins to run but before it ends, a person against whom the cause of action lies
-- confirms it, "the time during which the limitation period runs before the date of the confirmation does not count".
-- s 32 (2) (a): "a person confirms a cause of action if, but only if," he or she acknowledges the right or title, or makes
-- a payment in relation to it, to a person having the cause of action; s 32 (4): an acknowledgment must be "in writing and
-- signed by the maker"; s 32 (5), (6): "if, but only if" -- the benefit and the burden. The days before the confirmation
-- are added to the last day: the days on which the period ran before the date of the confirmation, that is, from the
-- day after time began (fork F1) to the day before the confirmation (fork F5).
DECLARE `What they did` IS ONE OF
    `acknowledged my right or title in writing, signed by them`
    `acknowledged my right or title, but not in writing or not signed`
    `made a payment to me in relation to my right or title`
    `neither`

@export Does an acknowledgment or a payment by the person I am claiming against give me more time (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 32)? If, while the time is running, they acknowledge your right in writing signed by them, or pay you something in relation to it, the time before that day does not count. The answer is your new last day (or the old one, if s 32 does not help).
GIVEN `time began` IS A DATE @desc The day the time limit began to run
      `last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day the Act would otherwise allow
      `what they did` IS A `What they did` @desc What the person you claim against did
      `confirmed on` IS A DATE @desc The day they did it
      `bound` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person you are suing made it, or is their successor (on death, bankruptcy or otherwise) after it was made, or a trustee of the same estate or trust, or later in possession of the property through them
      `to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was made to you, or to a person through whom you claim
GIVETH A DATE
`s 32 -- my last day after a confirmation` `time began` `last day` `what they did` `confirmed on` `bound` `to me` MEANS
    IF      (   `what they did` EQUALS `acknowledged my right or title in writing, signed by them`
             OR `what they did` EQUALS `made a payment to me in relation to my right or title`)
        AND `bound`
        AND `to me`
        AND `time began` `falls on or before` `confirmed on`
        AND `confirmed on` `falls on or before` `last day`
    THEN `the date` (`the days strictly between` `time began` `and` `confirmed on`) `days after` `last day`
    ELSE `last day`

-- s 33 (1): where the cause of action is based on fraud or deceit, or a relevant fact or the identity of the person
-- liable is deliberately concealed, "the time that elapses after a limitation period ... begins to run and before the date
-- when a person having ... the cause of action first discovers, or may with reasonable diligence discover, the fraud,
-- deceit or concealment ... does not count" against a person answerable for it. s 33 (3): a deliberate breach of duty
-- unlikely to be discovered for some time is deliberate concealment. s 33 (4): answerable "if, but only if" a party or a
-- successor. s 33 (5): not against an innocent purchaser for value.
DECLARE `What was hidden` IS ONE OF
    `my cause of action is based on fraud or deceit`
    `a fact relevant to my cause of action, or who is liable, was deliberately concealed`
    `a breach of duty was committed deliberately in circumstances where it was unlikely to be discovered for some time`
    `none of these`

-- s 33 (1), s 34 (1): "the time that elapses after a limitation period ... begins to run and before the date" of
-- discovery: from the day after time began (fork F1) to the day before discovery; those days are added (fork F5).
GIVEN `time began` IS A DATE, `last day` IS A DATE, `discovered on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day with the time before discovery not counted` `time began` `last day` `discovered on` MEANS
    IF `time began` `falls before` `discovered on`
    THEN `the date` (`the days strictly between` `time began` `and` `discovered on`) `days after` `last day`
    ELSE `last day`

@export Does fraud or concealment give me more time (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 33)? Where your claim is based on fraud or deceit, or a relevant fact or the identity of the wrongdoer was deliberately concealed, the time before you discovered it (or could have, with reasonable diligence) does not count against the person answerable for it. The answer is your new last day (or the old one, if s 33 does not help).
GIVEN `time began` IS A DATE @desc The day the time limit began to run
      `last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day the Act would otherwise allow
      `what was hidden` IS A `What was hidden` @desc What happened
      `answerable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person you are suing was a party to the fraud, deceit or concealment, or is their successor after it first occurred
      `innocent purchaser` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are suing someone who later bought the property for value without knowing or having reason to believe of the fraud, deceit or concealment (or someone claiming through them)
      `discovered on` IS A DATE @desc The day you first discovered it, or could with reasonable diligence have discovered it, whichever is earlier
GIVETH A DATE
`s 33 -- my last day after fraud or concealment` `time began` `last day` `what was hidden` `answerable` `innocent purchaser` `discovered on` MEANS
    IF      (NOT (`what was hidden` EQUALS `none of these`))
        AND `answerable`
        AND (NOT `innocent purchaser`)
    THEN `the last day with the time before discovery not counted` `time began` `last day` `discovered on`
    ELSE `last day`

-- s 34: for "a cause of action for relief from the consequences of a mistake", the time before the mistake is (or could
-- with reasonable diligence be) discovered does not count; not against an innocent purchaser for value (s 34 (3)).
@export Does a mistake give me more time (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 34)? Where your claim is for relief from the consequences of a mistake, the time before you discovered the mistake (or could have, with reasonable diligence) does not count. The answer is your new last day (or the old one, if s 34 does not help).
GIVEN `time began` IS A DATE @desc The day the time limit began to run
      `last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day the Act would otherwise allow
      `relief from a mistake` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is for relief from the consequences of a mistake (for example, to get back money paid by mistake)
      `innocent purchaser` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are suing someone who later bought the property for value without knowing or having reason to believe of the mistake (or someone claiming through them)
      `discovered on` IS A DATE @desc The day you first discovered the mistake, or could with reasonable diligence have discovered it, whichever is earlier
GIVETH A DATE
`s 34 -- my last day after a mistake` `time began` `last day` `relief from a mistake` `innocent purchaser` `discovered on` MEANS
    IF `relief from a mistake` AND (NOT `innocent purchaser`)
    THEN `the last day with the time before discovery not counted` `time began` `last day` `discovered on`
    ELSE `last day`

§§ `Part 3, Division 3.2 -- court extensions (ss 35-42)`

-- s 36 (1), (2): for "any action for damages if the damages claimed consist of or include damages in relation to personal
-- injuries", the court "may, if it decides that it is just and reasonable so to do, order that the period ... be
-- extended". s 36 (4): at any time, even after the period has ended or the action has begun. s 36 (5): "This section
-- does not apply in relation to a cause of action to which any of the following applies: (a) section 16AA ...; (b)
-- section 16B ...; (c) the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002, part 3.1". s 36 (6): nor to the period in s 30B (2). A flat
-- negative: the text's no.
DECLARE `Can the court extend the time under s 36` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court may extend the time, for the period it decides, if it decides that is just and reasonable (s 36 (2)), even after the time has run out or the action has begun (s 36 (4))`
    `no: s 36 does not apply to a claim to which s 16AA, s 16B or the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002, part 3.1 applies (s 36 (5))`
    `no: s 36 does not apply to the 6-year period in s 30B (2) (s 36 (6))`
    `no need: the claim has no time limit (s 21C)`

@export Can I ask the court to extend the time for my personal injury claim (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 36)? Only some time limits can be extended under s 36: the work injury limit (s 16A) and a child's health-service claim for a disease or disorder (s 30B (3)). Motor accident claims (s 16AA), other personal injury claims (s 16B) and death claims by relatives cannot (s 36 (5)); see ss 38 and 39 for claims after a death.
GIVEN `my claim` IS A `My injury claim` @desc What your claim is for
      `compensable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury is a compensable injury under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (ACT)
      `could claim compensation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you could make (or could have made) a workers compensation claim for it under that Act, if notice of the injury had been given as that Act requires
      `disease` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury is or includes a disease or disorder
GIVETH A `Can the court extend the time under s 36`
`s 36 -- can I ask the court to extend the time for my injury claim` `my claim` `compensable` `could claim compensation` `disease` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the Part 2 time limit for` `my claim` `compensable` `could claim compensation`
    WHEN `none: the action may be brought at any time (s 21C)` THEN `no need: the claim has no time limit (s 21C)`
    WHEN `s 16A: 3 years after the day the injury happened` THEN `yes: the court may extend the time, for the period it decides, if it decides that is just and reasonable (s 36 (2)), even after the time has run out or the action has begun (s 36 (4))`
    WHEN `s 30B: 6 years after the accident or, for a disease or disorder, the earlier of 6 years after the day of first knowledge and 12 years after the accident` THEN
        IF `disease`
        THEN `yes: the court may extend the time, for the period it decides, if it decides that is just and reasonable (s 36 (2)), even after the time has run out or the action has begun (s 36 (4))`
        ELSE `no: s 36 does not apply to the 6-year period in s 30B (2) (s 36 (6))`
    OTHERWISE `no: s 36 does not apply to a claim to which s 16AA, s 16B or the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002, part 3.1 applies (s 36 (5))`

-- s 37 (2): against a deceased person's estate, where the executor or administrator gave notice under the
-- Administration and Probate Act 1929 s 64 and distributed the assets without notice of the claim, "the court shall not
-- extend under this division the limitation period ... unless the estate is entitled to be indemnified ... by another
-- person or estate". A flat negative.
DECLARE `Does s 37 stop an extension` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court must not extend the time under Division 3.2 (s 37)`
    `no: s 37 does not stop the court extending the time`

@export If I am claiming against a dead person's estate, can the court still extend my time (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 37)? Not if the executor or administrator gave the statutory notice to claimants and then distributed the estate without notice of your claim, unless the estate is entitled to be indemnified by someone else.
GIVEN `against an estate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is against the estate of a deceased person
      `s 64 notice given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the executor or administrator gave notice under the Administration and Probate Act 1929 s 64 (the notice inviting claims)
      `distributed without notice of my claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they then distributed the estate's assets under that section without notice of your claim
      `indemnified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the estate is entitled to be indemnified for your claim by another person or estate (for example an insurer)
GIVETH A `Does s 37 stop an extension`
`s 37 -- does the estate's distribution stop an extension` `against an estate` `s 64 notice given` `distributed without notice of my claim` `indemnified` MEANS
    IF `against an estate` AND `s 64 notice given` AND `distributed without notice of my claim` AND (NOT `indemnified`)
    THEN `yes: the court must not extend the time under Division 3.2 (s 37)`
    ELSE `no: s 37 does not stop the court extending the time`

-- s 38 (1): where the executor or administrator sues on the deceased's own personal injury claim, the court may extend
-- the period "for the further period, not exceeding 6 years, beginning on the day of the person's death", if just and
-- reasonable. "Beginning on" a day counts that day (fork F2): the last day is the day before the sixth anniversary.
@export As executor or administrator, what is the latest the court can extend the time to for the deceased's own personal injury claim (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 38)? Up to 6 years beginning on the day of death, if the court considers it just and reasonable, whether or not the time had run out.
GIVEN `died on` IS A DATE @desc The day the injured person died
GIVETH A DATE
`s 38 -- the latest day the court can extend the estate's injury claim to` `died on` MEANS
    `the date` (0 MINUS 1) `days after` (`the date` 6 `years after` `died on`)

-- s 39: two routes for the relatives' death claim (s 16). (1), (2): where the deceased's own claim had run out against
-- them, the court may specify "a period not exceeding 6 years beginning on the day of death". (4): where the person had a
-- cause of action at death, the court may extend the s 16 period "not exceeding 6 years from the date of death". The two
-- are worded differently (finding 6): "beginning on" counts the day of death, "from" does not.
DECLARE `My death claim route` IS ONE OF
    `the deceased's own claim had run out of time before the death (s 39 (1))`
    `the deceased still had a cause of action at death (s 39 (4))`

@export As personal representative bringing a claim for a death, what is the latest the court can allow (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 39)? Up to 6 years after the death, if the court decides it is just and reasonable: where the deceased's own claim had already run out, a period beginning on the day of death (s 39 (1), (2)); otherwise an extension of the s 16 period from the date of death (s 39 (4)).
GIVEN `route` IS A `My death claim route` @desc Which situation you are in
      `died on` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
GIVETH A DATE
`s 39 -- the latest day the court can allow for the death claim` `route` `died on` MEANS
    CONSIDER `route`
    WHEN `the deceased's own claim had run out of time before the death (s 39 (1))` THEN `the date` (0 MINUS 1) `days after` (`the date` 6 `years after` `died on`)
    WHEN `the deceased still had a cause of action at death (s 39 (4))` THEN `the date` 6 `years after` `died on`

-- s 40 (1): for "latent damage to property or ... economic loss in relation to such damage", the court may extend the
-- period "for the further period not exceeding 15 years beginning on the day when the act or omission that gave rise to
-- the cause of action occurred", if just and reasonable.
@export What is the latest the court can extend the time to for a claim for latent damage to property, or economic loss from it (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 40)? Up to 15 years beginning on the day of the act or omission that caused it, if the court considers it just and reasonable.
GIVEN `act or omission on` IS A DATE @desc The day of the act or omission that gave rise to the claim (for example, the defective building work)
GIVETH A DATE
`s 40 -- the latest day the court can extend my latent damage claim to` `act or omission on` MEANS
    `the date` (0 MINUS 1) `days after` (`the date` 15 `years after` `act or omission on`)
